{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/622731-opening-a-caern-and-the-wyrm-s-attack-on-the-process-also-the-weaver","id":622731,"title":"Opening a Caern and the Wyrm's attack on the process. Also, the Weaver.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-08-10T15:48:43-07:00","id":622731,"title":"Opening a Caern and the Wyrm's attack on the process. Also, the Weaver.","content":"If the Garou open a Caern, it gets Auto-Attacked. My question is, which Wyrm minions attack, and how far away do they come from? Do Nosferatu in the sewers suddenly feel the urge to make a suicidal attack on a group of Shifters whose location they suddenly know? <br /> <br /> Or is it more, spirits and maybe Fomori that show up? And if so, how far away would you say they come from? It can't be Malfeas, obviously, because Malfeas could send an unlimited number of soldiers. So then that means local Hellholes. Of course that brings to mind another question. The Wyrm and Weaver are constantly at war, and if one empties itself of soldiers, they are leaving themselves open to a raid by the other. <br /> <br /> This leads me to my next question, is it a bad idea to tell the Weaver's forces in advance that you are about to open a Caern, and then inform the Banes in their Hellhole that in a few hours, if they leave their Hellhole, their home will be obliterated by the Weaver?<br /> <br /> I am asking since the idea, the more I look at it seems both daft and yet perhaps a standard procedure in some areas where both sides are strong and neither wants to step foot outside their territory lest they invite an attack on their turf.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-10T18:38:48-07:00","id":622982,"title":"","content":"Meh. I think you might be attributing a too-human sort of mindset on the various Wyrm-baddies. Certainly Black Spiral Dancers and certain Wyrm-cultists might think along those lines, but I've always viewed the massed charge of Banes as being more akin to a swarm of bees. Once they get riled up, they just mindlessly attack -- there's no real strategy or intelligence behind it. Sure some Banes are intelligent, but they're also <i>spirits</i> and thus bound very tightly by their natures. So even if they individually are capable of planning, opening a caern is something akin to running a lawn-mower underneath a hive of killer-bees. They can't help themselves -- instinct just takes over and they attack.<br /> <br /> None of this means that the Garou can't use that trait as a <i>weapon</i>, however. Do a Rite of Caern Opening, and set it up in advance so that you (or more accurately somebody <i>else</i>) takes down a Hive too. Two for the price of one.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/546/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/545/7z8m.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3506-uniform-two-six","name":"Uniform Two Six","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3506_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"455"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T00:37:01-07:00","id":623163,"title":"","content":"When my players did this I decided that the majority of the attack was made up of banes big and small. Many of them merged with the local wildlife in 'revenge' for the Caern's creation and made a bunch of mutant critters attack. There was also some weird spirit that might have been a high torment Fallen (ambiguity is the story-teller's friend), as well as a pack of vampires that felt inexorably drawn to the site (the undead weren't sure why, but some of them suspected it was an ancient calling out to them and they wanted to diablerize it).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3695-the-laughing-stranger","name":"The Laughing Stranger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3695_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1010"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T04:45:29-07:00","id":623218,"title":"","content":"The last time a group of players helped create a caern in a campaign was the final chapter of a Dark Ages Chronicle the attack involved corrupted mages, a massive bane spirit similar to a hydra, a Formori knightly order, and waves of lesser banes. <br /> I have no problem with throwing everything and the kitchen sink at players during such a dramatic and pivotal moment.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/293-lncbill","name":"lncbill","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar293_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-16T07:56:48-07:00","id":628643,"title":"","content":"Also, I wouldn't really ascribe as much rationality and amicability to the Weaver as that. Its goals are quite alien and inimical to our thinking. What use does the Weaver have of a gap in the Umbra? It's just a blight on its perfect order which should split the chaos of the umbra from the shape to come. So just let the partisans that make its job harder fight each other and continue with the Great Work. Better yet, come down and eliminate them both when they are tired of fighting each other and calcify the breach.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-16T15:56:42-07:00","id":629140,"title":"","content":"Alien, yes, but not stupid. Is it really the Weaver that is a head in the race for absolute control, or the Wyrm? The Wyrm and Wyld may not have the capacity to understand when they are losing or to calculate, but if more forces aren't brought to bear against the Wyrm, the Weaver might get eaten along with everything else.<br /> <br /> A temporary armistice in this situation would be wise, even among enemies. Making and breaking alliances is what being a conqueror is all about.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-16T16:27:34-07:00","id":629163,"title":"","content":"The Weaver isn't a conqueror. It is a mad spiritual force obsessed with control. That's the thing: all three are just maddening forces, Wyld included. Some of its servants can think it more straightforward, but most are just following urges, even Drones and Weaver Spirits.<br /> <br /> So, to begin with, they actually are mostly dumb.<br /> <br /> When you get the ideologically sided servants of the Weaver, they are a lot more likely to just let their enemies go for it. The Weaver IS strong. It ISN'T loosing to the Wyrm.<br /> <br /> It is so strong, actually, that one of the strong possibilities for the Apocalypse is that either the Weaver allies with the Wyrm or even wins alone in the end.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-16T22:34:39-07:00","id":629569,"title":"","content":"The Weaver may be strong, but its victory is far from certain. And when I think of the Weaver I think of a chess program that's being run by a super computer. It may have fewer pieces, but it is the paragon of strategy. Hell, the Weaver is the spiritual SOURCE of strategy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-17T02:19:57-07:00","id":629715,"title":"","content":"No. Strategy and chess are our own constructs. Weaver is stagnation, inaction and perfection of clockwork. Being a master planner requires you to think outside the box, and that's something that Weaver is conceptually incapable of.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-17T09:48:33-07:00","id":629990,"title":"","content":"Curiously the Weaver would be, indeed, an awesome chess player. But not because of strategy. Its because chess is a Solvable Game, like Tic Tac Toe. It seems overly complex and a strategical game for us because the sheer amount of combinations is far above what the human mind can process. But if processed, it has a solution, because from start you have everything laid before you. Every piece and rule, every movement. Chess is made of known information, with no random or hidden variables taking place. The Weaver would certainly be capable of dealing with the amount of computing needed to find the solution.<br /> <br /> But Strategy is about dealing with unknown variables. They can be unknown because they're external, hidden, or just outside calculation possibilities. But they are unknown, so you have to find a sub-optimal answer able to optimize chances instead of give certainty. The Weaver is all about certainty. It is about everything going precisely as planned. Obsessively ordered. And as any one with an obsession, it can't handle unexpected situations.<br /> <br /> Of course, it does have more strength in numbers and teamwork than the other could expect. It is reasonable that the forces of the Weaver are able to coordinate with perfection. But coordination itself isn't strategy. It still can loose. That's why the Weaver is maddened and dangerous. Not because it is a perfect strategist, but precisely because it ISN'T. If it was, there would be no need for it to change the world, because it would be able to predict the Wyld (quite a paradox) and the Wyrm and just do its work perfectly. Instead, it is forcing upon the world its order, to eliminate the &quot;chaos&quot; it can't even understand.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, the Weaver only even comprehends what it already possess. Lucky for it it is so strong now. But it is by sheer force, not strategy, no more than the other two.<br /> <br /> You focus on the war for the world as an actual game of War between three mighty generals, even if somewhat mad ones. It isn't really like that. Instead, it is unbalance. It is three spiritual forces out of control, each one obsessively doing just what they do, but spiraling toward catastrophe.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}